msgid "LOOP_HELP"
msgstr "A measure is a group of beats between vertical lines. From and Through include both end measures. Play selected section repeats them with your count-in setting."

msgid "HELP_STRINGS"
msgstr "Read guitar tablature (tabs) from left to right. The six lines represent strings. The top line is string 1, the thinnest. The bottom line is string 6, the thickest. Standard tuning is E–A–D–G–B–E from thickest to thinnest."

msgid "HELP_FRETS"
msgstr "A fret is a metal strip across the guitar neck. For a 3 on a tab line, press that string just behind the third fret, on the side closer to the tuning pegs, then pluck it. A 0 means pluck the open string without pressing. Stacked numbers ask for notes played together, called a chord."

msgid "HELP_STAFF"
msgstr "On the five-line staff, higher positions show higher pitches. A quarter note lasts as long as two eighth notes. A half note lasts as long as two quarter notes. In 4/4, count a beat for each quarter note. In 6/8, count two beats per measure, each as long as three eighth notes. A rest shows silence. A tie joins notes of the same pitch into one held sound. The guitar staff writes notes an octave higher than they sound, with the same note names one register up."

msgid "HELP_TIMING"
msgstr "A beat is the pulse you can count or tap. A measure, or bar, is the group of beats between vertical lines. Turn on Beat clicks in Playback to hear the pulse while the music plays. The rhythm display is simplified. Audio keeps the original timing."

msgid "HELP_TECHNIQUES"
msgstr "Open Score & print in Menu, then About this arrangement to choose Basic tab, Strum with a pick or Fingerpick. A strum bracket marks the strings to sweep. X means keep that string quiet. B marks a possible barre, where one finger presses several strings at one fret. For fingerpicking, T is thumb. I, M and R are the index, middle and ring fingers of the picking hand. Notes left out of the tab can still appear on the staff and sound in playback."

msgid "FOLLOW_HINT"
