msgid "SONG_DETAILS_META"
msgstr "%s · %s · practice tempo %d BPM · %s"

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_COMMON"
msgstr "The starting tempo is in quarter notes per minute. Canon in D uses Pachelbel’s repeating bass. The other bass lines were written for practice."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_ODE_TO_JOY"
msgstr "Start with E–E–F–G in bar 1. Give each note one beat. The 16-bar melody is in C. The first two phrases begin alike and end differently. Listen for quicker notes in the third phrase and a drop to low G before the opening returns."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_ODE_TO_JOY"
msgstr "Ludwig van Beethoven completed his Ninth Symphony in 1824. It premiered in Vienna on 7 May. In the finale, cellos and double basses introduce the tune before voices sing words from Friedrich Schiller’s poem “Ode to Joy”. The tune later became the wordless anthem of the Council of Europe and the European Union."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_FUR_ELISE"
msgstr "The opening tune leads into a contrasting phrase and returns to finish on A. The melody plays an octave lower for guitar. Near the return, it combines notes from both piano hands into one line. Listen for the high E and the quick alternation of E and D-sharp. This arrangement plays the opening section once."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_FUR_ELISE"
msgstr "Beethoven’s surviving sketches date from 1810. When he revisited the piece in 1822, he marked it as number 12. Ludwig Nohl first published it in 1867 under the title Für Elise, decades after Beethoven’s death. The finished manuscript Nohl described is lost, and Elise’s identity remains uncertain."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_SPRING"
msgstr "Play the opening of “Spring” in E, one octave below the violin part. In the original, the first phrase gets a softer answer, like an echo. This study keeps the repeated melody at a steady volume and closes on its own held E."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_SPRING"
msgstr "Antonio Vivaldi published “Spring” in 1725 as the first of the Four Seasons concertos, within his Opus 8 collection. Each concerto has an accompanying poem called a sonnet. In “Spring”, it describes birdsong, flowing streams and a thunderstorm. Directions in the printed parts match these scenes to passages in the music."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_CANON_IN_D"
msgstr "The first violin’s opening and its first faster variation are played twice, one octave lower. Listen for the melody changing from one note per beat to mostly two, while the eight bass notes repeat every two bars. Both parts hold D at the end. The lowest bass note, D, is below a standard guitar’s lowest open string."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_CANON_IN_D"
msgstr "Johann Pachelbel wrote this chamber work for three violins and a supporting bass part, pairing it with a lively dance called a gigue. The violins enter one after another with the same music over a repeating bass pattern. Its date and original occasion are unknown; Jean-François Paillard’s 1968 recording helped launch its modern popularity."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_TWINKLE"
msgstr "Repeat bar 1 to practice the jump from C to G. Both notes are played twice. The 12 bars form three four-bar sections. Listen for the opening to return in bar 9."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_TWINKLE"
msgstr "Jane Taylor’s words first appeared as “The Star” in Rhymes for the Nursery in 1806. The familiar melody is the French tune “Ah, vous dirai-je, Maman”. Its composer is unknown. Mozart wrote twelve keyboard variations on the tune, each using the same melody as a starting point."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_THE_ENTERTAINER"
msgstr "Two short lead-in notes take you into the first 16-bar section. Listen for notes that start between the beats and keep sounding across the next beat or bar. That shift in emphasis is called syncopation. At the end, a rest takes the place of the two notes that would lead into a repeat."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_THE_ENTERTAINER"
msgstr "Scott Joplin’s piano rag was published in 1902 with a dedication to James Brown and his mandolin club. Ragtime grew from African American musical traditions and places accents between the beats of a steady pulse. Publisher John Stark later issued a version for two mandolins and guitar. The 1973 film The Sting brought the tune to a new audience, and Marvin Hamlisch’s recording earned a Grammy."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_MARY_HAD_A_LITTLE_LAMB"
msgstr "This melody uses just C, D, E and G. Compare bars 1–2 and 5–6. Both start E–D–C–D, then the next bar gives a different answer. Hold the last C in bar 8 for four beats. The eight-bar tune plays twice."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_MARY_HAD_A_LITTLE_LAMB"
msgstr "Sarah Josepha Hale published “Mary’s Lamb” in 1830. The poem circulated through print and in New England Sunday schools. Thomas Edison later recalled using the rhyme to test his first phonograph in 1877."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_FRERE_JACQUES"
msgstr "The eight-bar melody plays twice. Listen for the quick bell phrase and the low G in the closing line. To try the round with a partner, turn off the practice bass and have the second person start the melody when the first reaches bar 3."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_FRERE_JACQUES"
msgstr "“Frère Jacques” is a French round: singers begin the same melody at different times, allowing its phrases to overlap. Long passed down as traditional children’s music, it has been attributed to Jean-Philippe Rameau by musicologist Sylvie Bouissou through her research into eighteenth-century French manuscripts of musical canons."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_AULD_LANG_SYNE"
msgstr "The verse and chorus are in C. A short low G leads into the verse. Listen for the long–short rhythms and the short A at the end of bar 9 that leads into the chorus. Hold the final C for four beats."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_AULD_LANG_SYNE"
msgstr "Robert Burns discussed this older Scots song in a 1788 letter to Frances Dunlop. The words he reworked first reached print with a different melody. George Thomson published them with the now-familiar tune in 1799. The title means roughly “for old time’s sake”. The song is often sung on Hogmanay, the Scottish New Year’s Eve."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_YANKEE_DOODLE"
msgstr "The verse and chorus each last eight bars, and the pair plays twice. The verse starts with even short notes. Listen for the long–short rhythm as the chorus begins in bar 9."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_YANKEE_DOODLE"
msgstr "The tune’s origins are uncertain. British soldiers used the song to mock American colonists, who turned it into a patriotic song by adding their own verses. In eighteenth-century Britain a “macaroni” was a man dressed in an exaggeratedly fashionable style. The feather verse jokes that one feather could make the singer fashionable."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_BRAHMS_LULLABY"
msgstr "Two short Es lead into the sung melody in C. Low C returns on the first beat of each full bar, following the repeating low note in Brahms’s piano accompaniment."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_BRAHMS_LULLABY"
msgstr "Johannes Brahms wrote his Wiegenlied, or cradle song, in 1868 for Bertha and Artur Faber’s baby son Hans. Originally for voice and piano, it appeared as Opus 49, No. 4. The first documented public performance was in Vienna in December 1869, with Louise Dustmann singing and Clara Schumann at the piano."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_MINUET_IN_G"
msgstr "Both 16-bar sections play once, an octave lower. Count three beats in each bar. The second section opens higher, moves toward D, then returns to G."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_MINUET_IN_G"
msgstr "Christian Petzold, an organist in Dresden, wrote this minuet for a keyboard suite. Anna Magdalena Bach copied it, together with a companion minuet in G minor, into her musical notebook. Long attributed to Johann Sebastian Bach, the piece is now credited to Petzold while retaining its familiar BWV appendix number."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_ROW_YOUR_BOAT"
msgstr "An eight-bar teaching version in C is played twice. Rhythm is simplified to quarter, half and whole notes, lasting one, two and four main beats. Try the separate lilting 1881 version for groups of three short notes. Both versions play one melody with a new practice bass, without overlapping round entrances."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_ROW_YOUR_BOAT"
msgstr "Versions of these words appeared in American sheet music in 1852 with a different melody. The familiar tune appeared in 1881 with a credit to E. O. Lyte, although that credit does not establish whether he composed or adapted it. As a round, the melody can overlap with itself when singers enter in turn."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_JINGLE_BELLS"
msgstr "A low G opens the verse. Listen for the leap up to E, then two steps back down. The familiar refrain begins at bar 17 with three repeated E notes. Its tune differs from the refrain in the 1857 print."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_JINGLE_BELLS"
msgstr "James Lord Pierpont published the song as “The One Horse Open Sleigh” in 1857. It reappeared in 1859 under the title “Jingle Bells, or the One Horse Open Sleigh”. The earliest documented performance was by Johnny Pell at Boston’s Ordway Hall in September 1857, in a blackface minstrelsy show built around racist caricatures of Black people."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_GREENSLEEVES"
msgstr "The familiar melody includes both sections and is lowered one octave. The long–short rhythms and held phrase endings are retained. Listen for the long high G at the start of bar 18 and the return to A at the end."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_GREENSLEEVES"
msgstr "“Greensleeves” is an anonymous tune from Renaissance England. Shakespeare refers to it in The Merry Wives of Windsor. One way musicians elaborated the melody was to play variations over a ground, a repeating bass pattern. The “Greensleeves to a Ground” concert notes explain this tradition."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_AMAZING_GRACE"
msgstr "The New Britain melody is in C. The short opening note comes before the first full bar. Listen for the high G held across bars 8 and 9, then the long final C in both the melody and bass."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_AMAZING_GRACE"
msgstr "John Newton prepared these words for a 1773 sermon in Olney, England; they appeared in Olney Hymns in 1779. William Walker paired them with the existing New Britain melody in his 1835 Southern Harmony. The melody's original composer remains uncertain. Judy Collins's recording entered the British singles chart in December 1970 and reached number five in 1971."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_LONDON_BRIDGE"
msgstr "The familiar version comes first. Listen for the lower, repeated notes when William Wells Newell’s version begins at bar 9. Both versions close with D–G–E–C. Newell’s final C is shorter and followed by a rest."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_LONDON_BRIDGE"
msgstr "This English singing game was also played in the United States by the nineteenth century. In an 1884 collection, folklorist William Wells Newell describes children making a bridge with raised arms and lowering it to catch players. His book preserves several versions with different words and rules."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_OLD_MACDONALD"
msgstr "The 16-bar classroom version in C includes the second farm-and-animal line before the repeated calls, then returns to the opening. A low G leads into the second line; some phrase endings leave a short rest. The practice bass is new. This is not a transcription of the earlier Ohio variant."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_OLD_MACDONALD"
msgstr "In 1917, F. T. Nettleingham’s collection of soldiers’ songs printed an early relative called Ohio, whose farmer is Old Macdougal. A 1925 Edison recording by the Sam Patterson Trio already uses the familiar title. The song survives in many versions, with different animals and sounds from verse to verse."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_WHEN_THE_SAINTS"
msgstr "This version uses an even four-beat pulse in C. The first three phrases all start with C–E–F. Listen for the third phrase to move away from G sooner than the first two."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_WHEN_THE_SAINTS"
msgstr "This African American spiritual became a fixture of New Orleans jazz. The Paramount Jubilee Singers recorded it in 1923. Blind Willie Davis sang it with only guitar in 1928. Louis Armstrong recorded Luis Russell’s arrangement in 1938, with Paul Barbarin’s parade-style drumming."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_AURA_LEE"
msgstr "The 1861 solo melody is moved from G to C. Note lengths are doubled to fit four-beat bars. Listen for the long then short opening notes and the small dip from C to B near the end. The practice bass follows some of the printed harmony changes. A small decorative note is skipped."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_AURA_LEE"
msgstr "George R. Poulton wrote the music and W. W. Fosdick the words. The 1861 Cincinnati print spells the title Aura Lea and dedicates the song to S. C. Campbell of Hooley & Campbell’s Minstrels. The same melody later became familiar in Elvis Presley’s Love Me Tender."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_SILENT_NIGHT"
msgstr "The familiar melody is in C and lowered one octave for guitar. Count two main beats per bar, with three eighth notes to each beat. The last C begins in bar 23 and continues into bar 24 as a single held note."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_SILENT_NIGHT"
msgstr "Joseph Mohr wrote the German words in 1816. Franz Xaver Gruber set them to music on Christmas Eve 1818, and the two performed it that evening in Oberndorf, near Salzburg. Mohr sang and played guitar, and Gruber sang the lower part. A surviving manuscript in Mohr’s hand shows the tune for two voices and guitar."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_CAMPTOWN_RACES"
msgstr "A short G before the first main beat leads into the verse. Its second half fits more notes into some beats. The refrain starts at bar 18 using the line labeled “Air”, meaning melody, in the 1850 score. The tune is lowered from D to C."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_CAMPTOWN_RACES"
msgstr "Stephen Foster wrote the words and music for Camptown Races, published in 1850. The cover advertises the Christy and Campbell Minstrels, troupes that performed blackface minstrelsy, a stage tradition built on racist caricatures of Black people. An edition for guitar appeared in 1852."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_AU_CLAIR_DE_LA_LUNE"
msgstr "This classroom version uses 16 bars in C. Listen for the lower, contrasting phrase at bar 9 and the return of the opening tune at bar 13."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_AU_CLAIR_DE_LA_LUNE"
msgstr "On 9 April 1860, Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville recorded a fragment of this French song with a phonautograph, which traced sound on soot-covered paper. First Sounds recovered audible sound from the trace in 2008. The Austrian Mediathek link plays the team’s improved 2010 restoration."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_HOT_CROSS_BUNS"
msgstr "Start with bars 1 and 2. Both play E–D–C, with the C held for two beats. In bar 3, fit four short Cs into two beats, then four short Ds into the next two. The four-bar pattern plays three times."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_HOT_CROSS_BUNS"
msgstr "This English nursery song echoes street sellers calling out buns for sale. A print from 1799 depicts the trade in London. The 1878 songbook The Baby’s Bouquet prints a longer version, with illustrations by Walter Crane and tunes collected and arranged by Lucy Crane."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_SIMPLE_GIFTS"
msgstr "Two short notes lead into the first section. The contrasting section begins at bar 10 with a held G. Each section plays once, and the tune comes to rest on C."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_SIMPLE_GIFTS"
msgstr "Simple Gifts is a Shaker dance song traditionally credited to Joseph Brackett of Maine. Its words connect bowing, bending and turning with the dance. Aaron Copland wrote a set of variations on the tune for Martha Graham’s ballet Appalachian Spring, which premiered at the Library of Congress in 1944."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_SAKURA_SAKURA"
msgstr "The melody follows Rudolf Dittrich’s 1894 Nippon Gakufu, lowered five semitones (piano-key steps). Listen for A–A–B at the start. The practice bass alternates E and B until the last bar, when both parts settle on E."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_SAKURA_SAKURA"
msgstr "Sakura means cherry blossoms. The song appears in an 1888 collection for koto, a Japanese stringed instrument whose strings run along a long wooden body. Later schoolbooks changed some words and notes. The tune’s earlier history and creators are not securely identified."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_POP_GOES_THE_WEASEL"
msgstr "The familiar eight bars lead into a higher dance section printed in 1883. Count two main beats to a bar, with three eighth notes in each beat. In bars 7 and 15, the short A is the pop. Leave the silence after it."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_POP_GOES_THE_WEASEL"
msgstr "Pop Goes the Weasel circulated as a country dance before becoming a children’s song. In an 1853 print, partners hold up joined hands and a dancer passes underneath while everyone sings the title line. Virginia fiddler Henry Reed was recorded playing the tune in 1966."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_HOME_ON_THE_RANGE"
msgstr "This verse and chorus are in C. Listen for the long high G that begins the chorus at bar 18, and the familiar phrase from the verse that returns near the end."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_HOME_ON_THE_RANGE"
msgstr "Brewster Higley’s poem My Western Home appeared in Kansas newspapers in the 1870s. Daniel Kelley and the Harlan family helped turn it into a song. In 1908, folklorist John Lomax recorded it from a Black saloonkeeper and former cowboy in San Antonio. The song appeared in his 1910 collection and became the Kansas state song in 1947."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_OH_SUSANNA"
msgstr "The 1848 Holt print uses two-beat bars. This teaching version in C gives each bar four beats and turns some even pairs into a long note followed by a short one. Listen for the opening of the refrain at bar 18. The practice version plays the melody and bass without lyrics."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_OH_SUSANNA"
msgstr "A Pittsburgh advertisement for a singing contest in 1847 announced Stephen Foster’s song as Susanna. Several printings appeared in 1848, including W. C. Peters’s edition prepared from Foster’s manuscript. The song originated in blackface minstrelsy, a stage tradition built on racist caricatures of Black people."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_PIANO_STUDY"
msgstr "In this eight-bar exercise in C major, the right hand plays one note per beat. The left hand plays on beats 1 and 3, with a repeated low C in the last bar."

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_PIANO_STUDY"
msgstr "LibreTabs wrote this piece as a first two-hand exercise. Try the right hand alone first, then add the bass."

