msgid "LIBRARY_ROW_YOUR_BOAT_COMPOUND"
msgstr "Row, Row, Row Your Boat · lilting 1881 · traditional"

msgid "LIBRARY_ROW_YOUR_BOAT_COMPOUND_NAME"
msgstr "Row Your Boat · lilting 1881"

msgid "LIBRARY_ROW_YOUR_BOAT_COMPOUND_CREDIT"
msgstr "traditional"

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_ROW_YOUR_BOAT_COMPOUND"
msgstr "This eight-bar melody comes from an 1881 songbook. It plays twice in C, with two groups of three eighth notes in each bar. Listen for the repeated-note pattern in bar 3. To try the round with a partner, start the second voice when the first reaches bar 3."

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_ROW_YOUR_BOAT_3"
msgstr "Franklin Square Song Collection — 1881, page 69"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_ROW_YOUR_BOAT_4"
msgstr "Library of Congress · 1852 Old Log Hut print"

msgid "LIBRARY_SKYE_BOAT_SONG"
msgstr "The Skye Boat Song · 1888 melody · Scottish traditional / A. C. MacLeod"

msgid "LIBRARY_SKYE_BOAT_SONG_NAME"
msgstr "The Skye Boat Song"

msgid "LIBRARY_SKYE_BOAT_SONG_CREDIT"
msgstr "Scottish traditional / A. C. MacLeod"

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_SKYE_BOAT_SONG"
msgstr "The song appeared in Songs of the North in the 1880s. It remembers Flora MacDonald helping Charles Edward Stuart reach Skye dressed as her maid after the 1746 Battle of Culloden. The printed score credits four bars to a tune Annie Campbell MacLeod collected from Hebridean boatmen and four more to MacLeod herself. Harold Boulton wrote the words."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_SKYE_BOAT_SONG"
msgstr "The arrangement follows an 1888 melody in G. Count two main beats in each bar, with three eighth notes to each beat. The solo melody begins at bar 9 with a different bass pattern. The chorus returns at bar 17."

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_SKYE_BOAT_SONG_1"
msgstr "Flora MacDonald: An unlikely Jacobite heroine — National Museums Scotland"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_SKYE_BOAT_SONG_2"
msgstr "The Music of the Waters — Laura Alexandrine Smith, 1888, pages 90–91"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_SKYE_BOAT_SONG_3"
msgstr "Songs of the North · melody and credits on page 16"

msgid "LIBRARY_LONG_LONG_AGO"
msgstr "Long, Long Ago · 1845 melody · Thomas Haynes Bayly"

msgid "LIBRARY_LONG_LONG_AGO_NAME"
msgstr "Long, Long Ago"

msgid "LIBRARY_LONG_LONG_AGO_CREDIT"
msgstr "Thomas Haynes Bayly"

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_LONG_LONG_AGO"
msgstr "Early printed editions credit this ballad to Thomas Haynes Bayly. The Morgan Library dates its Boston copy probably between 1835 and 1837; Connecticut College preserves a New York edition catalogued as 1845. These records do not establish a single certain first-publication year. Singer Elsie Baker recorded the song for Victor on 11 April 1913."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_LONG_LONG_AGO"
msgstr "The top sung line in the edition catalogued as 1845 is lowered from F to C. Listen for the quick falling notes and repeated low G in the contrasting phrase at bar 9. Short rests give each phrase room to breathe. The study begins with the tune and uses a new practice bass."

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONG_LONG_AGO_1"
msgstr "Long, long ago — early Boston edition, Morgan Library"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONG_LONG_AGO_2"
msgstr "Long Long Ago! — Firth & Hall edition, Connecticut College"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONG_LONG_AGO_3"
msgstr "Long, long ago — Elsie Baker, Victor recording, 1913, Library of Congress"

msgid "LIBRARY_DAISY_BELL"
msgstr "Daisy Bell · 1892 verse and chorus · Harry Dacre"

msgid "LIBRARY_DAISY_BELL_NAME"
msgstr "Daisy Bell · 1892 verse and chorus"

msgid "LIBRARY_DAISY_BELL_CREDIT"
msgstr "Harry Dacre"

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_DAISY_BELL"
msgstr "Harry Dacre’s song was published in 1892, during a bicycle craze. In 1961 Bell Labs used it to demonstrate computer singing. John Kelly and Carol Lochbaum programmed the voice and Max Mathews programmed the accompaniment. That recording inspired HAL’s rendition in 2001: A Space Odyssey."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_DAISY_BELL"
msgstr "One 32-bar verse leads into a 32-bar chorus, lowered from G to C. Start at bar 33 for the familiar chorus. Listen for three long notes descending from G to E to C. The chorus simplifies one small decoration from the 1892 edition."

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_DAISY_BELL_1"
msgstr "Daisy Bell — T. B. Harms, 1892, Levy Collection 140.090"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_DAISY_BELL_2"
msgstr "Singing Circuits — Library of Congress"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_DAISY_BELL_3"
msgstr "1961: computer-sung Daisy Bell — Computer History Museum"

msgid "LIBRARY_LONDONDERRY_AIR"
msgstr "Londonderry Air · 1855 (Danny Boy tune)"

msgid "LIBRARY_LONDONDERRY_AIR_NAME"
msgstr "Londonderry Air · 1855 melody"

msgid "LIBRARY_LONDONDERRY_AIR_CREDIT"
msgstr "Irish traditional · supplied by Jane Ross"

msgid "SONG_HISTORY_LONDONDERRY_AIR"
msgstr "An air is a melody. George Petrie published this Irish air without a title in his 1855 collection, crediting Jane Ross of Limavady for supplying it. Its composer is unknown. Listen for the wide range and long phrases in Danny Boy, a 1913 setting of the same tune with words by Frederic Edward Weatherly."

msgid "SONG_ARRANGEMENT_LONDONDERRY_AIR"
msgstr "The tune from Petrie’s 1855 print is lowered from E-flat to C. Listen for the low G-sharp in bar 7 and the high E in bar 14. A tie joins two written notes into one sound. Hold the tied closing C for two and a half beats."

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONDONDERRY_AIR_1"
msgstr "Petrie’s Ancient Music of Ireland — 1855, page 57"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONDONDERRY_AIR_2"
msgstr "Irish Folk Music and Song — Trinity College Dublin"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONDONDERRY_AIR_3"
msgstr "Danny Boy — 1913 edition, Connecticut College"

msgid "SONG_SOURCE_LONDONDERRY_AIR_4"
msgstr "Petrie’s collection and publication context — Irish Traditional Music Archive"

